WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.378
Picture leaving a helper in charge of your shop for the morning.

2
00:00:06.378 --> 00:00:08.315
You hand over a written checklist.

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:09.734
Open the stock room.

4
00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:12.524
Count yesterday's visitors in the door book.

5
00:00:12.524 --> 00:00:15.249
Then leave a note that says done or failed.

6
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.327
At lunch, the note says done.

7
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:20.421
Later you learn the stock room key never turned.

8
00:00:20.421 --> 00:00:21.960
The helper just moved on.

9
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:22.882
Nobody lied.

10
00:00:22.882 --> 00:00:26.249
The checklist never said what to do when a step fails.

11
00:00:26.249 --> 00:00:28.369
So here is the question for today.

12
00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:32.485
When a script ends, how does anyone know whether it really worked?

13
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:35.094
A script is that helper.

14
00:00:35.094 --> 00:00:39.095
It is a file of instructions the computer follows in order.

15
00:00:39.095 --> 00:00:42.553
Ours is logsum, a small Python script from the lab.

16
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:45.665
Its door book is a web server's access log.

17
00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:48.187
One line for every visit, or request.

18
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.967
When it finishes, it reports back on three separate channels.

19
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.356
The report sheet is standard output, or stdout.

20
00:00:55.356 --> 00:00:57.139
The real results go there.

21
00:00:57.333 --> 00:01:00.846
The note for the manager is standard error, or stderr.

22
00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:06.115
That is for messages meant for people: warnings, and the reason something failed.

23
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.977
And the thumbs up or down at the end is the exit code.

24
00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:10.733
One number.

25
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:12.038
Zero means success.

26
00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.061
Any other number means some kind of failure.

27
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.877
Now meet the most important reader of all.

28
00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:22.467
CI, the robot that runs your script every time someone changes the code.

29
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.405
It does not read the report.

30
00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:26.019
It does not read the note.

31
00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.255
It looks at one number, and decides.

32
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.702
A very busy manager.

33
00:01:29.702 --> 00:01:31.786
It only ever looks at the thumb.

34
00:01:31.786 --> 00:01:35.759
So if that number is wrong, nothing else you printed matters.

35
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:38.479
Let's run it on the lab's log.

36
00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.429
uv is the tool that runs our Python scripts.

37
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.343
The helper reads 238 requests.

38
00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:47.087
11 of them were server errors.

39
00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:48.916
That is 4.6 percent.

40
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.309
The limit is 1 percent unless you say otherwise.

41
00:01:52.309 --> 00:01:54.875
So what exit code should it hand back?

42
00:01:54.875 --> 00:01:55.956
Pause and guess.

43
00:01:58.133 --> 00:01:58.465
One.

44
00:01:58.465 --> 00:02:02.115
The check found a problem, and CI turns red.

45
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:04.372
The report went to stdout.

46
00:02:04.372 --> 00:02:12.712
And the one-line reason went to stderr: the error rate, 4.6 percent, is above the limit of 1.0 percent.

47
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:17.994
Raise the limit to 5 percent with a flag, and the same log passes.

48
00:02:17.994 --> 00:02:19.159
Exit code zero.

49
00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:20.247
CI goes green.

50
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:24.165
Why keep the report and the note apart?

51
00:02:24.165 --> 00:02:27.762
Because the report is often read by another program.

52
00:02:27.762 --> 00:02:31.428
Here, jq, a small tool that picks values out of JSON.

53
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.553
JSON is a plain text format that programs use to swap data.

54
00:02:36.553 --> 00:02:39.995
With format json, stdout holds only JSON.

55
00:02:39.995 --> 00:02:44.612
So jq reads it cleanly, and finds the error rate: 4.62.

56
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.098
Now say the helper uses print for a cheerful starting message.

57
00:02:49.098 --> 00:02:50.692
print writes to stdout.

58
00:02:50.692 --> 00:02:54.228
So the first line jq sees is: starting dot dot dot.

59
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.437
And jq fails.

60
00:02:55.437 --> 00:02:58.787
One friendly hello broke the whole report.

61
00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:00.624
So the rule is simple.

62
00:03:00.624 --> 00:03:03.159
Messages for people go to stderr.

63
00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:05.695
The report stays alone on stdout.

64
00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:07.526
Now the big trap.

65
00:03:07.526 --> 00:03:11.266
You might think the safest script is one that never crashes.

66
00:03:11.266 --> 00:03:15.318
So let's wrap the whole thing in try, and except Exception, pass.

67
00:03:15.318 --> 00:03:17.437
Catch every error, and do nothing.

68
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.260
Give it a log file that is not there.

69
00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.411
Pause and guess.

70
00:03:21.411 --> 00:03:22.633
What does CI see?

71
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:25.328
A zero.

72
00:03:25.328 --> 00:03:25.781
Green.

73
00:03:25.781 --> 00:03:30.985
The file was never read, nothing was checked, and the note says done.

74
00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:34.511
That is the stuck key from the opening, written in Python.

75
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:39.495
Honestly, except Exception pass should have been named: close your eyes and smile.

76
00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:41.788
Jokes aside, here is the point.

77
00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:44.800
A hidden failure looks exactly like success.

78
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.288
The same trap waits when a script asks another program for help.

79
00:03:50.288 --> 00:03:55.848
subprocess.run starts git, the tool that saves versions of files, and waits for it.

80
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.323
Git fails, say with a merge conflict.

81
00:03:58.323 --> 00:04:00.645
run only writes git's exit code down.

82
00:04:00.645 --> 00:04:04.788
And your script carries on to the next line, as if git had worked.

83
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:07.976
Add check=True, and the failure becomes loud.

84
00:04:07.976 --> 00:04:13.181
Python raises CalledProcessError, and the script stops instead of pretending.

85
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:18.050
So the lab gives every script the same contract for that number.

86
00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.015
Zero, all good.

87
00:04:19.015 --> 00:04:21.009
One, the check found a problem.

88
00:04:21.009 --> 00:04:23.389
Two, the script could not do its job.

89
00:04:23.533 --> 00:04:27.392
The real logsum catches FileNotFoundError by name.

90
00:04:27.392 --> 00:04:31.946
It writes one clear line on stderr, no such file, nope.log.

91
00:04:31.946 --> 00:04:33.644
And it hands back two.

92
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:38.484
Now a caller can tell the error rate is too high apart from the log is missing.

93
00:04:38.484 --> 00:04:40.619
Different trouble, different number.

94
00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:43.459
Three habits keep the helper honest.

95
00:04:43.459 --> 00:04:47.828
Catch the errors you expect, by name, and turn each one into a clear line.

96
00:04:47.828 --> 00:04:53.260
Let everything else crash, with its traceback, the trail of lines that shows where it broke.

97
00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:58.043
And when you call another program, pass a list, check equals true, and a timeout.

98
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:00.085
So back to the shop.

99
00:05:00.085 --> 00:05:02.044
The report goes on the sheet.

100
00:05:02.044 --> 00:05:03.868
The reasons go in the note.

101
00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:05.828
And the verdict is the thumb.

102
00:05:05.828 --> 00:05:08.260
A good helper says failed, out loud.

103
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.262
Quick check before you go.

104
00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:13.555
A script prints all fine, then exits with one.

105
00:05:13.555 --> 00:05:14.415
Did it pass?

106
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:16.864
No.

107
00:05:16.864 --> 00:05:20.027
CI trusts the number, not the words.
